{"name":"Melon Water","hex":"#fdbcb4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Some of the plants of the family Cucurbitaceae grown for food, generally not including the cucumber.\nGenus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others.\nGenus Citrullus, the watermelon and others.\nBenincasa hispida, the winter melon.\nMomordica charantia, the bitter melon.","Genus Cucumis, the true melon (including various cultigens like honeydew and cantaloupes), the horned melon, and others."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light pinkish orange color, like that of melon flesh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melon"},{"term":"water","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inorganic compound (of molecular formula .mw-parser-output .template-chem2-su{display:inline-block;font-size:80%;line-height:1;vertical-align:-0.35em}.mw-parser-output .template-chem2-su>span{display:block;text-align:left}.mw-parser-output sub.template-chem2-sub{font-size:80%;vertical-align:-0.35em}.mw-parser-output sup.template-chem2-sup{font-size:80%;vertical-align:0.65em}H2O) found at room temperature and pressure as a clear liquid; it is present naturally as rain, and found in rivers, lakes and seas; its solid form is ice and its gaseous form is steam.","The liquid form of this substance: liquid H2O."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To wet or supply with water; to moisten; to overflow with water; to irrigate.\n\n  To pour water into the soil surrounding (plants).\n\n  To provide (animals) with water for drinking.","To pour water into the soil surrounding (plants)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melon","blocks":[{"text":"A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit \" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/330px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon","wikidata":"Q5881191"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/1280px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Watermelon and melon in India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/500px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/1280px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/500px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Horned melon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/500px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/1280px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png/500px-Cantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Honeydew","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACantaloupe_Melon_cross_section.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Painted_green_melons._Chennai%2C_India%2C_2010.jpg/500px-Painted_green_melons._Chennai%2C_India%2C_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o